+\revminor{Within the same hour, total precipitation is the sum of its large-scale and convective parts, $\mathrm{tp}=\mathrm{lsp}+\mathrm{cp}$, so lsp is a lower bound on tp only for that hour; in the regional series, lsp exceeded tp by at most $3.4\times10^{-4}$~mm, at numerical precision. This bound does not carry across the forecast horizon, because the latest lsp input covers $(t_0-1\,\mathrm{h},t_0]$ and the target covers $(t_0+5\,\mathrm{h},t_0+6\,\mathrm{h}]$. Among the 430 training forecast origins whose latest lsp was at least 0.7~mm, the target was below that lsp value in 87.9\% of cases and below 0.1~mm in 39.8\%. Summed over all 26,300 retained hourly accumulations from 2004--2021, including dry hours, lsp accounted for 61.95\% of regional-mean tp (DJF 71.99\%, MAM 60.14\%, JJA 50.92\%, SON 64.50\%). Lagged lsp therefore describes recent, largely large-scale precipitation and its possible persistence; it does not constrain the target. Its incremental utility is assessed by the feature ablations in Section~\ref{sec:ablation_results}.}
 
