 \ReviewerComment{2. I have not found the answer why you exactly want to estimate total precipitation from ERA5 based on large-scale total precipitation and other predictors from ERA5? I can image that, except for summer, most of precipitation in Ireland is large-scale, maybe sometimes it is close to 100\% (you can easily check it, I am curious). So it gives the model already large piece of information. For example, if lsp=0.7 mm, the model knows that tp cannot be lower than 0.7. Or am I wrong?}
 
-\PendingResponse
+\AuthorResponse{We thank the reviewer for this question. The reviewer is right for the same hour, but the bound does not carry across the forecast horizon in our design. Within one hour, ERA5 total precipitation is the sum of large-scale and convective precipitation, $\mathrm{tp}=\mathrm{lsp}+\mathrm{cp}$, so lsp is a lower bound on tp for that hour. In our regional series, lsp exceeded tp by at most $3.4\times10^{-4}$~mm, which is numerical precision. However, all inputs, including lsp, are valid at or before the forecast origin $t_0$: the latest lsp value is the accumulation over $(t_0-1\,\mathrm{h},t_0]$, whereas the target is regional-mean tp over $(t_0+5\,\mathrm{h},t_0+6\,\mathrm{h}]$. The target-hour lsp is never an input. Following the reviewer's example, among the 430 training forecast origins whose latest lsp was at least 0.7~mm, the target was below that value in 87.9\% of cases and below 0.1~mm in 39.8\%. Lagged lsp therefore describes recent precipitation and its possible persistence; it does not tell the model a lower limit for the target.
 
-\ChangeLocation
+We also checked the large-scale share, as the reviewer suggested. Summed over all 26,300 retained regional-mean hourly accumulations from 2004--2021, including dry hours, lsp accounted for 61.95\% of tp: 71.99\% in DJF, 60.14\% in MAM, 50.92\% in JJA and 64.50\% in SON. These are ratios of summed lsp to summed tp, not means of hourly ratios. The reviewer's expectation is therefore confirmed in direction: large-scale precipitation dominates outside summer, and summer is close to an even split. The share is, however, well below 100\% in every season.
+
+The incremental value of lsp and the other precipitation inputs was assessed by retraining with predictors removed. In BiLSTM-Attention, removing lsp alone, or mxtpr and mntpr while retaining lsp, changed RMSE little. Removing mxtpr, mntpr and lsp together increased RMSE by 0.004805~mm\,h$^{-1}$ overall and by 0.019359~mm\,h$^{-1}$ in the upper tail, positive in all five seeds. In BU-LSTM-Attention, removing lsp alone slightly reduced overall RMSE, by 0.000870~mm\,h$^{-1}$ (pointwise moving-block bootstrap interval excluding zero; seed-$t$ interval including zero). The three lagged precipitation inputs therefore carry overlapping, modest predictive information rather than a large share of the answer. We have added a short paragraph to Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset} that states the same-hour identity, the timing that prevents it from bounding the target, and the share statistics. The choice of whether convective precipitation should also be an input is addressed in our response to Major Comment~3.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:dataset} (Dataset and Feature Selection): one paragraph added after the input-timing paragraph, giving the same-hour identity $\mathrm{tp}=\mathrm{lsp}+\mathrm{cp}$, the reason it does not bound the six-hour target, the 0.7~mm example on training origins and the seasonal large-scale share. Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:ablation_results} (group ablation) and Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:predictor_complementarity} (interpretation of the precipitation inputs) are cited unchanged. No Supplementary table was added; the share and lower-bound checks are recomputed from the regional-mean data without training.}
+
